00:30:48,222 --> 00:30:50,224
You. Get out of here.

488
00:30:50,766 --> 00:30:53,185
You go home and
you don't come back here!

489
00:30:55,604 --> 00:30:57,148
I don't want to see you.

490
00:30:59,650 --> 00:31:00,651
Ally, come inside!

491
00:31:07,950 --> 00:31:09,827
Ally, I'm in the kitchen.

492
00:31:12,788 --> 00:31:14,123
I'll do it.

493
00:31:17,960 --> 00:31:18,961
What?

494
00:31:25,051 --> 00:31:26,719
Cut! That was mint!

495
00:31:27,553 --> 00:31:30,848
Oh, my God. With the train...
So good!

496
00:31:30,931 --> 00:31:32,475
- Joe, reload the cap gun.
- Okay.

497
00:31:32,558 --> 00:31:34,977
We'll shoot another angle next.
Alice, that was mint.

498
00:31:35,061 --> 00:31:36,854
Martin, go a little faster next time.

499
00:31:37,229 --> 00:31:40,649
I'll try, Charles, but I'm still crying about
last night, man. I feel like I'm crazy.

500
00:31:40,816 --> 00:31:42,985
Guys, did it really look good?
My death?

501
00:31:43,069 --> 00:31:45,154
- Dude, it looked awesome.
- That was magic.

502
00:31:45,863 --> 00:31:47,656
What do you think happened?

503
00:31:48,783 --> 00:31:50,451
You mean why he crashed the train?

504
00:31:51,952 --> 00:31:53,788
I just got a horrible feeling.

505
00:31:59,418 --> 00:32:01,670
Dude, what are you doing?

506
00:32:14,517 --> 00:32:16,602
That whole thing's an Air Force train.

507
00:32:16,977 --> 00:32:17,978
What?

508
00:32:18,354 --> 00:32:21,482
I make models. Like, plastic ones.
You know, glue them, paint them...

509
00:32:21,565 --> 00:32:24,360
- And he's not embarrassed by that.
- Look who's talking, Math Camp!

510
00:32:24,443 --> 00:32:26,612
Thanks. Can I have a coffee, please?
Cream on the side.

511
00:32:26,695 --> 00:32:28,030
- Sure.
- He's so sophisticated.

512
00:32:28,114 --> 00:32:30,533
- Shut up. I like coffee.
- No one likes coffee.

513
00:32:30,616 --> 00:32:33,369
Air Force trains, even the models,
have these hooks...

514
00:32:33,452 --> 00:32:36,288
They do! For when the trains are
loaded on the transport ships.

515
00:32:36,372 --> 00:32:37,957
Every car in that crash had hooks.

516
00:32:38,040 --> 00:32:39,834
Joe, would you stop
talking about it, all right?

517
00:32:39,917 --> 00:32:40,960
Wait, guys.

518
00:32:41,043 --> 00:32:43,462
Am I the only one who doesn't
understand what any of this means?

519
00:32:43,546 --> 00:32:44,713
- Probably, Smartin.
- Cary, shut up.

520
00:32:44,964 --> 00:32:46,340
- You shut up.
- I don't like it when you call me that.

521
00:32:46,423 --> 00:32:48,050
I'm sorry, Smartin.
Let's just go cry about it.

522
00:32:48,300 --> 00:32:52,721
Dr. Woodward had that map.
He drove onto the train tracks.

523
00:32:52,805 --> 00:32:55,057
Maybe there was something
he wanted to...

524
00:32:55,182 --> 00:32:57,309
- To destroy, yeah!
- My God, will you guys just shut up?

525
00:32:57,393 --> 00:32:59,728
Maybe he was just sick of being old
and wanted to kill himself.

526
00:32:59,812 --> 00:33:00,980
That's a dumb idea.

527
00:33:01,063 --> 00:33:02,314
He had a gun. Why not use that?

528
00:33:02,398 --> 00:33:06,610
There are infinitely more effective ways
to commit suicide. Pills, hanging...

529
00:33:06,819 --> 00:33:08,737
Pussy! Stop taking the fries away.

530
00:33:08,946 --> 00:33:10,865
- I ordered these for a reason.
- Excuse me.

531
00:33:10,948 --> 00:33:13,450
Could we get another order of fries?
Because my friend here is fat.

532
00:33:13,534 --> 00:33:16,579
Funny, chompers. At least I don't have
to use a booster seat.

533
00:33:16,745 --> 00:33:18,372
You could jump from a building.

534
00:33:18,581 --> 00:33:21,041
- Or fall down some stairs.
- Well, that's not really effective.

535
00:33:21,292 --> 00:33:24,003
If it's the Air Force, what would
the Air Force have on the train?

536
00:33:24,086 --> 00:33:25,588
Jesus, shut up about it.

537
00:33:26,380 --> 00:33:29,383
You heard what Old Man Woodward
said, we can't talk about this.

538
00:33:29,633 --> 00:33:32,094
Joe, seriously.
Do you really wanna take a chance

539
00:33:32,178 --> 00:33:33,846
that something could happen
to your dad, too?

540
00:33:42,855 --> 00:33:44,398
All right, easy, easy!

541
00:33:49,111 --> 00:33:50,946
- It's loaded. Move out.
- Yes, sir.

542
00:33:54,408 --> 00:33:57,703
Yes, Deputy. I'm told this crash has
caused a whole mess of confusion.

543
00:33:57,786 --> 00:33:59,413
Well, as you can expect,
the local authorities

544
00:33:59,496 --> 00:34:01,957
are trying to figure out just what
happened, as well as how to help you.

545
00:34:02,041 --> 00:34:04,293
It's all under control.
It'll be a very fast clean-up.

546
00:34:04,418 --> 00:34:06,170
Colonel, there isn't anything
that I should know, is there?

547
00:34:06,295 --> 00:34:08,214
Not that I can think of, Deputy.

548
00:34:08,297 --> 00:34:10,507
I understand you have concerns
about our cargo.

549
00:34:10,633 --> 00:34:12,343
Well, I'd like to see that manifest, yes.

550
00:34:12,635 --> 00:34:14,136
Well, that's not gonna be possible.

551
00:34:14,303 --> 00:34:15,804
Colonel, I've got a lot of people
in my town

552
00:34:15,888 --> 00:34:17,723
that are gonna wanna know
what's taking place.

553
00:34:18,224 --> 00:34:19,516
Which town is that?

554
00:34:19,600 --> 00:34:20,684
Lillian.

555
00:34:22,686 --> 00:34:24,647
Deputy, if you're asking me if we had
any dangerous property

556
00:34:24,730 --> 00:34:27,733
on board this train,
I can assure you the answer is "No."

557
00:34:28,234 --> 00:34:29,235
Colonel!

558
00:34:29,610 --> 00:34:30,694
Excuse me.

559
00:34:42,122 --> 00:34:43,207
They gave me the runaround
for two hours

560
00:34:43,290 --> 00:34:44,667
before they allowed me
to talk to anyone.

561
00:34:44,750 --> 00:34:46,669
I'd say they've had their hands full, Jack.

562
00:34:46,752 --> 00:34:48,879
Nelec, the Colonel, refused to
show me the manifest.

563
00:34:48,963 --> 00:34:51,715
He told me they're carrying mostly
airplane parts. I don't buy that.

564
00:34:51,799 --> 00:34:54,635
It's Air Force, Jack.
Airplane parts sort of make sense.

565
00:34:54,927 --> 00:34:57,137
Sheriff, I'm telling you,
there's something else going on.

566
00:34:57,221 --> 00:35:00,808
They're taking molds of tire tracks.
Why would they be doing that?

567
00:35:00,891 --> 00:35:02,101
Who would they be looking for?

568
00:35:02,184 --> 00:35:04,853
And they're packaging these
weird little cubes into these crates.

569
00:35:04,979 --> 00:35:05,938
Weird little cubes?

570
00:35:06,021 --> 00:35:09,108
Yeah, they're loading them onto these
red trucks. There are dozens of them.

571
00:35:09,191 --> 00:35:11,151
Sheriff, I think you need to go
check it out.

572
00:35:11,235 --> 00:35:12,695
And I think you need to take a break.

573
00:35:13,696 --> 00:35:15,906
Jack, I've been telling you this
since Elizabeth.

574
00:35:15,990 --> 00:35:18,367
You're a good deputy,
but you need to take a vacation.

575
00:35:19,076 --> 00:35:23,080
Go home. Take off the uniform.
Go fishing. Give your son a hug.

576
00:35:38,345 --> 00:35:41,682
Hey, Breen, I'm not sure Edie's
paying you to listen to the radio.

577
00:35:43,726 --> 00:35:44,727
Breen!

578
00:35:45,894 --> 00:35:47,229
Hey, Sheriff.

579
00:35:47,396 --> 00:35:48,480
What's that?

580
00:35:48,731 --> 00:35:52,359
Walkman. It's like a stereo.
Play your own cassette tapes.

581
00:35:52,693 --> 00:35:53,694
You want to try?

582
00:35:53,819 --> 00:35:55,487
I don't think so.

583
00:35:55,571 --> 00:35:58,574
Kids walking around with their
own stereos, just what we need.

584
00:35:59,366 --> 00:36:00,743
It's a slippery slope, my friend.

585
00:36:01,160 --> 00:36:02,494
Yes, sir.

586
00:37:42,886 --> 00:37:44,138
Sheriff?

587
00:37:50,727 --> 00:37:51,979
Sheriff?

588
00:38:43,572 --> 00:38:45,741
You know, in scene... Where is it?

589
00:38:46,909 --> 00:38:48,243
Where'd you learn to do this?

590
00:38:48,577 --> 00:38:50,245
Mostly the <i>Dick Smith
Monster Make-up Handbook.</i>

591
00:38:50,329 --> 00:38:52,789
Hey, Joe. We need some more
footage of the train crash.

592
00:38:52,873 --> 00:38:53,916
Obviously.

593
00:38:53,999 --> 00:38:55,501
You know that train model
you just made?

594
00:38:55,584 --> 00:38:57,711
- Your cargo train.
- I want to blow it up and film it.

595
00:38:57,920 --> 00:38:59,588
Let me blow it up.

596
00:39:00,589 --> 00:39:02,257
Yeah. Sure.

597
00:39:02,549 --> 00:39:03,759
- Mint!
- Gnarly! Yes!

598
00:39:05,385 --> 00:39:06,386
Here, take this, will you?

599
00:39:08,263 --> 00:39:12,226
It's fine. They can blow it up.
I don't care.

600
00:39:14,520 --> 00:39:16,355
You want to see it? The train?

601
00:39:16,605 --> 00:39:18,941
My train model? The train I made?

602
00:39:21,235 --> 00:39:22,236
Dude, that's bitching.

603
00:39:22,319 --> 00:39:23,695
Dude, that's like the third time
it's happened.

604
00:39:23,779 --> 00:39:24,821
What the hell?

605
00:39:24,905 --> 00:39:27,074
So how am I supposed to be a zombie?

606
00:39:30,619 --> 00:39:34,289
Pretty much just be a lifeless ghoul,
with no soul. Dead eyes.

607
00:39:34,623 --> 00:39:37,251
Scary. Did you ever have Mrs. Mullin?

608
00:39:37,459 --> 00:39:39,127
- For English? Yeah.
- Yeah.

609
00:39:39,336 --> 00:39:41,755
Kind of like her,
but hungry for human flesh.

610
00:39:41,964 --> 00:39:44,132
Like she wants to turn
somebody into a zombie.

611
00:39:44,299 --> 00:39:46,176
'Cause that's kind of what zombies do.

612
00:39:59,815 --> 00:40:01,900
Oh, my God, yeah. That's really good.

613
00:40:20,544 --> 00:40:21,878
Not bad.

614
00:40:22,045 --> 00:40:23,880
- Really?
- Really.

615
00:40:33,599 --> 00:40:38,270
Careful! There was a gas spill.
I don't even know how much fuel I lost.

616
00:40:38,353 --> 00:40:40,230
You don't seem to be asking
very many questions.

617
00:40:40,314 --> 00:40:42,024
Give the man some time, Mr. Blakely.

618
00:40:42,190 --> 00:40:44,651
- He's only a deputy, he's not the Sheriff.
- Doesn't matter.

619
00:40:45,193 --> 00:40:48,196
I think this is most likely a bear attack.

620
00:40:48,322 --> 00:40:50,991
Security cameras showed nothing.
Someone had erased...

621
00:40:51,074 --> 00:40:53,243
Vicky, you heard
from Sheriff Pruitt this morning?

622
00:40:53,327 --> 00:40:55,162
<i>Negative. Still no word.</i>

623
00:40:59,875 --> 00:41:02,419
Vicky, you need to have Dayton
send a C.S. unit out to Edie's,

624
00:41:02,502 --> 00:41:04,713
and you put an A.P.B. out on the Sheriff
and Breen Haskell,

625
00:41:04,796 --> 00:41:07,591
and send units over to their houses
immediately. You copy all that?

626
00:41:07,674 --> 00:41:12,054
<i>Copy. And, Jack,
we're getting a lot of unusual calls.</i>

627
00:41:12,512 --> 00:41:14,056
What kinds of unusual calls?

628
00:41:14,640 --> 00:41:16,350
I've never seen this kind of thing before.

629
00:41:16,433 --> 00:41:18,060
I mean,
what the hell am I supposed to do?

630
00:41:18,143 --> 00:41:21,355
The insurance company guy
thought I was joking with him.

631
00:41:21,438 --> 00:41:23,398
Look at my Bonneville over here!

632
00:41:23,523 --> 00:41:25,317
They'd have to have
a winch to take that out.

633
00:41:25,400 --> 00:41:27,611
You been having any
troubles with anyone lately, Izzy?

634
00:41:28,111 --> 00:41:30,614
Well, I was thinking
you should talk to Louis Dainard.

635
00:41:31,323 --> 00:41:34,451
Yeah. I didn't help him out
with financing last year,

636
00:41:34,534 --> 00:41:38,080
and I wouldn't be surprised if this was
some sort of sick retribution.

637
00:41:38,163 --> 00:41:40,707
- My generator is missing.
- The phones aren't working.

638
00:41:40,791 --> 00:41:43,585
- My dog's gone. Disappeared.
- The phones won't produce a signal.

639
00:41:43,669 --> 00:41:45,045
Call the station and file a report.

640
00:41:45,128 --> 00:41:47,756
I'll have someone come out
and take care of each one of you. Okay?

641
00:42:08,443 --> 00:42:10,112
Lucy!

642
00:42:12,864 --> 00:42:14,449
Lucy!

643
00:42:43,478 --> 00:42:45,188
I got four guys on this case...

644
00:42:45,272 --> 00:42:47,733
People don't feel safe around here.
You know why? Because they're not.

645
00:42:47,816 --> 00:42:48,900
We need to get the National Guard...

646
00:42:48,984 --> 00:42:50,402
Air Force hasn't
answered a damn question.

647
00:42:50,485 --> 00:42:52,237
They've got trucks over at
Woodward's house.

648
00:42:52,320 --> 00:42:54,114
Now, Ben heard they were looking
for Woodward's research.

649
00:42:54,197 --> 00:42:55,198
Research? What the hell?

650
00:42:55,282 --> 00:42:57,367
...shotgun in one of those trucks,
he had, like, a scanner in his hand.

651
00:42:57,451 --> 00:42:58,410
Like a Geiger counter.

652
00:42:58,493 --> 00:43:00,203
They're saying it's just clean up
at the crash site, too,

653
00:43:00,287 --> 00:43:01,955
but they're taking molds of tire tracks.

654
00:43:02,038 --> 00:43:04,624
That means they're looking for
someone. Now what does that mean?

655
00:43:08,712 --> 00:43:10,213
Have you seen Lucy anywhere?

656
00:43:10,964 --> 00:43:12,966
No. I'm sure
she's around somewhere.

657
00:43:13,049 --> 00:43:15,177
I put a couple of slices
in the fridge for you there.

658
00:43:16,261 --> 00:43:18,096
I ate those.

659
00:43:18,180 --> 00:43:20,599
- Sorry, kid.
- Yeah, no problem. I'll find something.

660
00:43:20,682 --> 00:43:22,017
- Hey!
- You got to be kidding.

661
00:43:24,853 --> 00:43:26,188
Pick that up, will you?

662
00:43:28,732 --> 00:43:30,567
Hey, Vicky,
you got power down at the station?

663
00:43:30,650 --> 00:43:32,444
<i>On and off
And Brook County's in the dark.</i>

664
00:43:32,527 --> 00:43:35,155
Do me a favor and get Water & Power
out there right now, will you?

665
00:43:35,363 --> 00:43:38,325
So, the Air Force is looking for a car.
Alice's dad's car.

666
00:43:38,533 --> 00:43:41,286
They're taking tire prints
from the crash site. Over.

667
00:43:41,369 --> 00:43:42,662
I don't want to hear about it.

668
00:43:42,746 --> 00:43:45,707
This is what I've been talking about.
They could be monitoring us right now.

669
00:43:45,832 --> 00:43:48,710
Jesus, shut up. Over.

670
00:43:48,919 --> 00:43:51,755
And the military is at Woodward's house
looking for his research.

671
00:43:51,838 --> 00:43:53,089
Whatever that means.

672
00:43:53,173 --> 00:43:54,633
You cannot flake out on me
and be a dick.

673
00:43:54,716 --> 00:43:56,551
Are you gonna come and
help me tomorrow or not?

674
00:43:56,760 --> 00:43:58,678
<i>I need you to make Alice
a zombie again.</i>

675
00:43:58,762 --> 00:44:00,388
Okay. I'll do the make-up.

676
00:44:01,139 --> 00:44:02,307
<i>Over.</i>

677
00:44:06,436 --> 00:44:09,314
<i>The cables are gone.
All the poles are empty.</i>

678
00:44:09,439 --> 00:44:13,443
<i>Same thing over here in Rose Hill.
Somebody took the damn wires down.</i>

679
00:44:13,527 --> 00:44:15,111
<i>Holy shit!</i>

680
00:44:15,403 --> 00:44:17,614
<i>Hey, Rooney,
you got anything in Lillian?</i>

681
00:44:17,697 --> 00:44:19,199
<i>Hey, Rooney, you copy?</i>

682
00:44:19,324 --> 00:44:21,284
We got a half mile of copper missing.

683
00:44:21,785 --> 00:44:23,328
Lines are back up at M-38.

684
00:44:23,662 --> 00:44:27,582
<i>Truck 14, Rooney's out at the junkyard.
I'll give you an update when I hear back.</i>

685
00:45:40,322 --> 00:45:41,907
<i>I wanna know who took them.</i>

686
00:45:42,324 --> 00:45:44,034
Twenty microwaves

687
00:45:44,451 --> 00:45:46,536
gone from my inventory.

688
00:45:46,870 --> 00:45:48,830
Everywhere you look,
things are going missing.

689
00:45:49,080 --> 00:45:51,625
We've got trains crashing,
people vanishing.

690
00:45:51,708 --> 00:45:53,835
Belmont County's without power.

691
00:45:54,002 --> 00:45:57,505
You know what this feels like to me?
This feels like a Russian invasion.

692
00:45:59,799 --> 00:46:01,968
Debbie, I don't think that the Russians
have anything to do

693
00:46:02,052 --> 00:46:03,678
with what's going on in town.

694
00:46:03,762 --> 00:46:05,931
But I am calling the curfew because
of these exact concerns.

695
00:46:06,014 --> 00:46:08,600
Unless you can tell me who's been
messing around with our stuff,

696
00:46:08,683 --> 00:46:10,185
I say it's the Soviets.

697
00:46:11,186 --> 00:46:14,314
I like you, Jack,
but we need Sheriff Pruitt back.

698
00:46:15,106 --> 00:46:17,525
We've got good people working on that.

699
00:46:18,151 --> 00:46:19,277
Mr. McCandless.

700
00:46:23,365 --> 00:46:26,660
Since Wednesday morning,
my ham radio is a mess!

701
00:46:26,868 --> 00:46:28,912
Some frequencies which I like to scan,

702
00:46:29,621 --> 00:46:33,458
I'm hearing military chatter,
but it's distorted.

703
00:46:33,541 --> 00:46:35,543
And I don't know what it is.
Have you heard that?

704
00:46:35,627 --> 00:46:37,921
Mr. McCandless, can I have a word
with you in private, please?

705
00:46:38,380 --> 00:46:40,548
It was like having my radio hijacked.

706
00:46:40,882 --> 00:46:41,925
I'm sure that must be very annoying.

707
00:46:42,008 --> 00:46:43,635
You don't know those frequencies
offhand, do you?

708
00:46:43,718 --> 00:46:45,887
Sure I do. 13-2-0-1 ...

709
00:46:46,930 --> 00:46:48,598
Patty, why don't you get
in touch with Vicky.

710
00:46:48,682 --> 00:46:49,891
Hey, Dave, you seen Milner?

711
00:46:49,975 --> 00:46:51,059
No, sir. I haven't.

712
00:46:51,142 --> 00:46:52,477
Jack, there's a huge sinkhole
by my garage!

713
00:46:52,560 --> 00:46:53,728
- It sunk almost a foot!
- I'll take care of that.

714
00:46:53,812 --> 00:46:56,648
Hey, Tom. Tom! You seen Milner?

715
00:46:56,773 --> 00:46:58,608
Have you tried his office?

716
00:46:59,109 --> 00:47:00,443
Have I tried his office?

717
00:47:00,735 --> 00:47:02,862
What are you gonna do about the
power? It's been out for two days.

718
00:47:04,114 --> 00:47:05,448
Hey, Rosko.

719
00:47:06,282 --> 00:47:08,118
Rosko. I need you to get
every radio we have

720
00:47:08,201 --> 00:47:09,202
and you tune them to those frequencies.

721
00:47:09,285 --> 00:47:10,328
- Understand?
- No.

722
00:47:10,412 --> 00:47:11,413
- Hey, Milner.
- Yeah.

723
00:47:11,496 --> 00:47:13,540
Don't you have a radio scanner
with a descrambler?

724
00:47:13,623 --> 00:47:15,125
- A voice inverter, yeah, at home.
- Whatever. Get it.

725
00:47:15,208 --> 00:47:16,835
Bring it down here and set it up for me.
Would you please?

726
00:47:16,918 --> 00:47:18,003
- Sure thing.
- Jack, what is this?

727
00:47:18,086 --> 00:47:20,255
I think the Air Force is using
unassigned channels...

728
00:47:20,338 --> 00:47:23,216
Hey. He was at my house
yesterday morning. You aware of that?

729
00:47:23,299 --> 00:47:25,260
We brought him in for the car lot
and other stuff. He's clean.

730
00:47:25,343 --> 00:47:29,180
Well, I'd appreciate it if you'd inform
your son that my house is off-limits.

731
00:47:29,764 --> 00:47:31,766
And so is my daughter.

732
00:47:36,021 --> 00:47:38,356
I think the make-up
was better yesterday.

733
00:47:38,815 --> 00:47:40,316
No, it's good.

734
00:47:41,317 --> 00:47:42,610
I think it looks good.

735
00:47:44,112 --> 00:47:45,280
I should've cleaned up my room.

736
00:47:50,285 --> 00:47:52,203
Did you make all these models?

737
00:47:53,121 --> 00:47:57,792
Yeah. That one is
the Hunchback of Notre Dame.

738
00:47:58,168 --> 00:48:00,670
We watched that one on TV.

739
00:48:08,053 --> 00:48:10,889
This is the train they want to destroy.

740
00:48:11,806 --> 00:48:13,016
Looks real.

741
00:48:14,309 --> 00:48:15,977
It looks so old.

742
00:48:16,394 --> 00:48:18,396
Yeah, that's dry brush technique.

743
00:48:20,482 --> 00:48:23,651
It's when there's a coat of paint
and then you put something

744
00:48:23,735 --> 00:48:25,612
like Euro Gray over it.

745
00:48:25,737 --> 00:48:27,072
Euro Gray?

746
00:48:27,405 --> 00:48:30,784
Oh, my God, there's like
14 different grays. It gets crazy.

747
00:48:37,248 --> 00:48:39,000
What was that necklace?

748
00:48:39,334 --> 00:48:42,087
The one you were holding
at the train crash.

749
00:48:42,170 --> 00:48:43,213
Was it your mom's?

750
00:48:46,674 --> 00:48:49,094
My dad gave it to her the day I was born.

751
00:48:50,095 --> 00:48:51,471
She wears it all the time.

752
00:48:52,180 --> 00:48:53,681
Well, used to.

753
00:48:53,932 --> 00:48:55,058
And...

754
00:48:58,353 --> 00:49:00,855
It was bad how she died and...

755
00:49:01,523 --> 00:49:03,066
But my dad got it back.

756
00:49:07,362 --> 00:49:09,364
Guys, what the hell?
No one knew where you were!

757
00:49:09,697 --> 00:49:10,740
We're here.

758
00:49:10,865 --> 00:49:13,368
- You got to play the soldier.
- I thought you were calling Evan.

759
00:49:13,618 --> 00:49:15,203
Evan blew us off. He's a pussy!

760
00:49:17,122 --> 00:49:18,289
Excuse me.

761
00:49:18,373 --> 00:49:19,457
It's okay.

762
00:49:19,541 --> 00:49:21,334
You got to do it. You're the only one.

763
00:49:21,417 --> 00:49:23,294
Well, what the hell you mean they found
my dog in Brookville?

764
00:49:23,378 --> 00:49:24,671
That's another county over!

765
00:49:25,213 --> 00:49:27,841
Lucy. That's where she is.
I mean, we've got nearly 30 calls

766
00:49:27,924 --> 00:49:29,175
from people who found local dogs,

767
00:49:29,259 --> 00:49:31,594
but the thing is
the calls coming in aren't local.

768
00:49:33,888 --> 00:49:37,642
It's like they all just ran away
in every direction.

769
00:49:38,476 --> 00:49:40,728
This isn't like her,
she just doesn't disappear.

770
00:49:40,854 --> 00:49:43,148
Okay, okay, calm down.
Just tell me what she looks like.

771
00:49:43,231 --> 00:49:45,733
She's got dark hair. It was in rollers.
Do you know what rollers are?

772
00:49:45,859 --> 00:49:47,652
- Yes, I know what rollers are.
- Okay, so...

773
00:49:47,735 --> 00:49:48,862
Hang on.

774
00:49:50,071 --> 00:49:52,407
<i>Negative, unable to recover data.</i>

775
00:49:52,824 --> 00:49:53,992
That's definitely Air Force.

776
00:49:54,242 --> 00:49:55,660
Yeah, it's Air Force,
but what does it mean?

777
00:49:55,743 --> 00:49:58,163
What are they doing transmitting
on these frequencies?

778
00:49:58,246 --> 00:50:00,832
- Maybe it's not even Air Force.
- I bet you five bucks it's Air Force.

779
00:50:00,915 --> 00:50:02,167
- Five bucks?
- Yeah.

780
00:50:02,250 --> 00:50:05,420
<i>We're gonna need units to prepare for
Operation Walking Distance.</i>

781
00:50:05,753 --> 00:50:07,088
What did he say? "Operation" what?

782
00:50:07,380 --> 00:50:08,756
"Walking Distance."

783
00:50:09,632 --> 00:50:10,675
What's this?

784
00:50:10,758 --> 00:50:12,719
He worked at Romero Chemical.

785
00:50:12,802 --> 00:50:14,762
Found out some things
the company was doing.

786
00:50:14,929 --> 00:50:18,266
And after what you told me last night
at the bar, I thought you should know.

787
00:50:19,684 --> 00:50:21,519
Are we gonna get in trouble
for being here?

788
00:50:21,603 --> 00:50:22,770
Production value.

789
00:50:22,854 --> 00:50:25,523
Stop talking about production value.
I think the Air Force is gonna kill us.

790
00:50:25,607 --> 00:50:27,400
- Cary.
- Don't cover my mouth!

791
00:50:27,483 --> 00:50:30,403
We just made this discovery today.
You understand this is top secret?

792
00:50:30,486 --> 00:50:31,529
Of course.

793
00:50:31,613 --> 00:50:33,406
I would never have given you
this information

794
00:50:33,489 --> 00:50:35,116
unless we had worked
together in Vietnam.

795
00:50:35,283 --> 00:50:37,493
- Those were hard times.
- I'd rather not talk about it.

796
00:50:37,660 --> 00:50:38,661
Understood.

797
00:50:39,329 --> 00:50:40,997
- You're a good friend.
- You, too.

798
00:50:42,165 --> 00:50:43,499
Nothing so far.

799
00:50:43,833 --> 00:50:46,794
- How much more is there?
- About 60 more boxes in the basement.

800
00:50:47,128 --> 00:50:48,796
What, more tax returns?

801
00:50:49,130 --> 00:50:53,301
It's here somewhere. We'll find it. If not,
Woodward will tell us where it is.

802
00:50:53,635 --> 00:50:55,094
What the hell is that?

803
00:51:00,141 --> 00:51:01,142
Joe.

804
00:51:02,268 --> 00:51:03,686
Hey, Mr. Lamb.

805
00:51:04,979 --> 00:51:06,272
Get in the car.

806
00:51:11,945 --> 00:51:15,990
Mr. Lamb, that's your camera,
but, technically, that's my film.

807
00:51:22,956 --> 00:51:23,957
What the hell?

808
00:51:25,124 --> 00:51:26,334
Is it me?

809
00:51:26,417 --> 00:51:28,044
I would like to help you out, Deputy,
I really would,

810
00:51:28,336 --> 00:51:30,129
but we operate on
a need-to-know basis.

811
00:51:30,213 --> 00:51:31,506
Why are your trucks sweeping the town?

812
00:51:31,589 --> 00:51:32,966
We will be out of your way shortly.

813
00:51:33,049 --> 00:51:36,010
All right, then I'm sure
you won't mind me contacting D.C.

814
00:51:36,261 --> 00:51:38,221
Talk to some friends about
"Walking Distance."

815
00:51:41,724 --> 00:51:43,017
All right, we'll do it that way.

816
00:51:44,018 --> 00:51:46,187
Deputy! Let's talk.

817
00:51:46,688 --> 00:51:48,022
Just not here.

818
00:51:58,157 --> 00:52:00,827
This is new, all of this,
for the both of us.

819
00:52:01,619 --> 00:52:04,122
Dealing with anything. Just us.

820
00:52:04,330 --> 00:52:07,834
So, I'm gonna make this
as simple and as clear as I can.

821
00:52:08,084 --> 00:52:09,836
You're not friends with Alice Dainard.

822
00:52:10,169 --> 00:52:14,340
When I say "No," I don't mean "Maybe."
I don't mean "Yes."

823
00:52:14,799 --> 00:52:16,009
I mean "No."

824
00:52:16,384 --> 00:52:20,179
I've known Louis Dainard for a lot
of years. He's been nothing but trouble.

825
00:52:20,596 --> 00:52:23,016
Your mother used to say he's
not such a bad guy.

826
00:52:23,099 --> 00:52:25,059
He just needs a chance,
that he was sad.

827
00:52:25,518 --> 00:52:26,728
Well, I tried to be good to him.

828
00:52:27,103 --> 00:52:28,730
And I can't, not anymore.

829
00:52:30,315 --> 00:52:32,942
I will not allow him
or his daughter in this house.

830
00:52:33,026 --> 00:52:35,153
I will not allow you to
spend time with her,

831
00:52:35,236 --> 00:52:38,197
doing projects or whatever it is
that you do. That's it.

832
00:52:38,281 --> 00:52:39,949
I hope we're clear on that.

833
00:52:42,327 --> 00:52:43,911
We're not clear.

834
00:52:45,038 --> 00:52:45,997
What'd you say?

835
00:52:46,080 --> 00:52:47,081
We're not clear.

836
00:52:47,165 --> 00:52:50,376
You and I aren't clear about anything.
We couldn't be less clear.

837
00:52:50,501 --> 00:52:54,756
Just because Mom died doesn't mean
you know anything about me. You don't.

838
00:52:54,922 --> 00:52:57,842
You don't know anything about
Alice, either. She's kind.

839
00:52:57,925 --> 00:52:59,052
We're not gonna have this discussion
right now.

840
00:52:59,135 --> 00:53:01,304
- She's nice to me!
- I don't care what she is!

841
00:53:02,930 --> 00:53:05,475
Her father is an irresponsible,
selfish son of a bitch.

842
00:53:06,851 --> 00:53:09,103
You listen to me.
I've got 12,000 people in this town

843
00:53:09,187 --> 00:53:12,857
who are scared out of their mind.
They've got one person to rely on.

844
00:53:13,107 --> 00:53:15,693
It used to be someone else,
but now it's just me.

845
00:54:56,461 --> 00:54:57,462
Move, move!

846
00:54:57,670 --> 00:54:58,671
Let's go. Move!

847
00:54:58,754 --> 00:55:01,466
<i>Deputy, drop your weapons.
Put your hands on the car.</i>

848
00:55:01,549 --> 00:55:02,925
I'm here to see Colonel Nelec!

849
00:55:03,050 --> 00:55:05,052
He told me to meet him here.

850
00:55:05,219 --> 00:55:07,388
<i>You're under military arrest.
Drop your weapons.</i>

851
00:55:07,763 --> 00:55:09,390
Where's Nelec?

852
00:55:11,559 --> 00:55:14,187
I don't want any more trouble, do you?

853
00:55:17,899 --> 00:55:21,360
We've been going through your
belongings, looking for evidence.

854
00:55:21,444 --> 00:55:25,364
What you knew. How you knew it.
Who else might know.

855
00:55:34,457 --> 00:55:36,083
I remember you in the lab.

856
00:55:36,417 --> 00:55:38,294
And I recall we didn't always see
eye to eye,

857
00:55:38,377 --> 00:55:41,088
but that is no excuse
for doing what you did here.

858
00:55:41,714 --> 00:55:46,052
So, in the spirit of moving forward
and avoiding more trouble,

859
00:55:46,135 --> 00:55:48,763
why don't you just tell me
where you put your research

860
00:55:48,846 --> 00:55:50,181
and who else knows about it.

861
00:55:50,473 --> 00:55:53,559
I know you had someone document
the crash that night.

862
00:55:53,726 --> 00:55:55,770
Tell me who that was
and I will help you.

863
00:55:56,395 --> 00:55:57,897
I swear to God on my mother's life,

864
00:55:57,980 --> 00:56:00,942
if you help us now,
we will take care of you.

865
00:56:03,110 --> 00:56:05,988
I've seen what happens, Mr. Nelec,

866
00:56:07,823 --> 00:56:10,117
- when you take care of people.
- It is mine.

867
00:56:10,201 --> 00:56:11,702
He is mine.

868
00:56:12,328 --> 00:56:14,747
I'm gonna bait him and catch him.

869
00:56:15,164 --> 00:56:17,959
He's in me, you know.

870
00:56:20,628 --> 00:56:22,296
As I am in him.

871
00:56:23,172 --> 00:56:24,382
So,

872
00:56:26,133 --> 00:56:27,468
when you see him next,

873
00:56:28,553 --> 00:56:30,555
as I'm sure you will,

874
00:56:31,430 --> 00:56:33,474
I'll be watching you, too.

875
00:57:18,060 --> 00:57:20,396
Are you... Were you sleeping?

876
00:57:20,771 --> 00:57:23,524
Before. Earlier. No.

877
00:57:25,276 --> 00:57:26,569
Are you okay?

878
00:57:26,652 --> 00:57:28,195
Yeah, I'm fine.

879
00:57:30,281 --> 00:57:31,282
You want to come in?

880
00:57:31,365 --> 00:57:32,533
Yeah.

881
00:57:40,333 --> 00:57:41,542
Power's still out.

882
00:57:43,628 --> 00:57:45,129
Room's still messy.

883
00:57:45,212 --> 00:57:46,547
I couldn't sleep.

884
00:57:47,423 --> 00:57:48,966
I was thinking.

885
00:57:50,468 --> 00:57:53,054
I wanted to tell you something
before tomorrow.

886
00:57:54,847 --> 00:57:56,849
Don't let Charles blow up your train.

887
00:57:59,060 --> 00:58:00,770
I don't think it's right.

888
00:58:01,896 --> 00:58:04,857
I know he's your friend.
He's so bossy.

889
00:58:04,940 --> 00:58:07,777
But I've known him since kindergarten.
He's really nice.

890
00:58:07,860 --> 00:58:10,488
He shouldn't always get what he wants.

891
00:58:10,905 --> 00:58:13,282
I mean, who always gets
what they want?

892
00:58:16,744 --> 00:58:19,080
I know I don't know you at all.

893
00:58:19,580 --> 00:58:22,416
Even though it sort of feels like I do.

894
00:58:27,254 --> 00:58:30,549
Do you not feel like that?

895
00:58:30,925 --> 00:58:32,718
No. No, I totally do.

896
00:58:33,260 --> 00:58:36,514
I'm just kind of in shock
at this entire conversation.

897
00:58:40,768 --> 00:58:41,852
Power's back.

898
00:58:41,936 --> 00:58:43,562
No, no. Keep it.

899
00:59:27,523 --> 00:59:29,316
It's... It's so weird.

900
00:59:30,985 --> 00:59:34,363
Watching her like this.
Like she's still here.

901
00:59:38,993 --> 00:59:44,665
She used to look at me this way,
like really look,

902
00:59:48,502 --> 00:59:52,465
and I just knew I was there,
that I existed.

903
01:00:07,021 --> 01:00:08,856
He drank that morning.

904
01:00:10,149 --> 01:00:11,358
My dad.

905
01:00:14,862 --> 01:00:16,655
He missed his shift.

906
01:00:26,499 --> 01:00:28,501
Your mom took it for him,

907
01:00:29,043 --> 01:00:30,586
the day of the accident.

908
01:00:51,941 --> 01:00:53,067
He...

909
01:00:55,027 --> 01:00:56,111
He...

910
01:00:56,612 --> 01:00:58,364
He... He wishes...

911
01:01:00,115 --> 01:01:02,952
I know he wishes it was him
instead of her.

912
01:01:08,123 --> 01:01:10,084
And sometimes I do, too.

913
01:01:14,213 --> 01:01:16,173
Don't say that.

914
01:01:18,467 --> 01:01:20,219
He's your dad.

915
01:01:42,491 --> 01:01:44,285
Is... Is this normal?

916
01:01:46,120 --> 01:01:47,997
No. This is new.

917
01:02:39,423 --> 01:02:41,759
Want to tell me where you were?

918
01:02:44,428 --> 01:02:46,221
Or should I tell you?

919
01:02:54,605 --> 01:02:55,689
Sit down.

920
01:02:56,774 --> 01:02:58,192
I should go to bed.

921
01:02:58,609 --> 01:03:00,194
I want to talk to you. I said sit down.

922
01:03:00,277 --> 01:03:01,820
I don't want to sit down!

923
01:03:03,697 --> 01:03:04,949
Then go.

924
01:03:05,866 --> 01:03:08,369
Be just like your mother and leave.

925
01:03:10,037 --> 01:03:11,205
Dad...

926
01:03:11,705 --> 01:03:12,915
Go ahead! Go!

927
01:03:15,042 --> 01:03:16,043
Go!

928
01:03:22,716 --> 01:03:23,717
Alice!

929
01:03:24,134 --> 01:03:25,302
Alice!

930
01:03:25,678 --> 01:03:26,845
Alice!

931
01:03:27,972 --> 01:03:28,973
Wait!

932
01:03:52,871 --> 01:03:54,123
Ally, I'm sorry!

933
01:04:24,778 --> 01:04:25,946
Alice!

934
01:04:28,198 --> 01:04:29,241
Ally!

935
01:04:33,787 --> 01:04:34,955
Alice!

936
01:04:35,289 --> 01:04:36,415
Ally!

937
01:04:45,758 --> 01:04:46,842
Ally!

938
01:04:50,804 --> 01:04:53,432
<i>Firelight at 0500 hours.</i>

939
01:04:53,557 --> 01:04:56,310
<i>Operation Walking Distance is in effect.</i>

940
01:04:56,393 --> 01:04:59,229
<i>T-minus four hours to evacuation.</i>

941
01:04:59,313 --> 01:05:01,940
<i>Evacuation in T-minus four hours.</i>

942
01:05:02,649 --> 01:05:05,235
Why are you not reacting?
That thing went through my wall!

943
01:05:05,319 --> 01:05:06,612
I told you not to pick it up
in the first place.

944
01:05:06,695 --> 01:05:09,239
What are you so angry about? That I'm
not gonna let you blow up my train?

945
01:05:09,323 --> 01:05:11,742
That's part of it. Just a part.

946
01:05:12,242 --> 01:05:16,288
Hey. Tell your sister that Donny
from Olsen's broke up with Karen.

947
01:05:16,663 --> 01:05:19,083
That shit ended about a week ago.

948
01:05:21,418 --> 01:05:22,419
What's your problem?

949
01:05:22,503 --> 01:05:24,963
My whole movie's a disaster
because of you.

950
01:05:25,047 --> 01:05:27,883
- I know my dad's being a turkey.
- Whatever.

951
01:05:28,675 --> 01:05:31,845
- We're still gonna finish your movie.
- It's not about the movie.

952
01:05:31,929 --> 01:05:33,514
What are you talking about?
Of course it is!

953
01:05:33,597 --> 01:05:34,848
Just forget it.

954
01:05:34,932 --> 01:05:38,477
Why? There's nothing wrong!
We're gonna finish the movie!

955
01:05:52,741 --> 01:05:55,285
I can get the camera back.
I know where my dad put it.

956
01:05:55,369 --> 01:05:56,912
We can still make the festival.

957
01:05:56,995 --> 01:05:58,956
- It's not about the movie.
- Then what is it about?

958
01:05:59,039 --> 01:06:01,125
Jesus, you don't get it, do you?
It's obvious.

959
01:06:01,208 --> 01:06:03,127
- What's obvious?
- God, you're a dumb-ass.

960
01:06:03,210 --> 01:06:05,212
That night of the crash,
you started getting all weird.

961
01:06:05,295 --> 01:06:08,006
- What? What are you talking about?
- Like Mr. Attitude, all of a sudden.

962
01:06:08,090 --> 01:06:10,968
Your movie was over!
That's what you said! You were upset!

963
01:06:11,051 --> 01:06:12,928
- Oh, my God.
- I gave you a camera! I helped you!

964
01:06:13,011 --> 01:06:14,471
Don't pretend like you did this for me.

965
01:06:14,555 --> 01:06:16,056
You didn't do this for me,
and you know it.

966
01:06:16,765 --> 01:06:20,018
This was never about me.
This was always about Alice!

967
01:06:20,102 --> 01:06:21,228
<i>Action!</i>

968
01:06:21,311 --> 01:06:23,021
<i>I'm going to stay here and investigate.</i>

969
01:06:23,105 --> 01:06:26,316
<i>I think it's safer
if you leave town for a couple of days.</i>

970
01:06:26,400 --> 01:06:29,361
<i>John, I don't like it.
